Live runtime setup attempts in this turn:

1. Workspace-local private tmux socket:
   mkdir -p .live/tmux
   TMUX_TMPDIR=.live/tmux tmux -L fm-lab new-session -d -s probe -x 120 -y 40 'sleep 30'
   Result: error connecting to {run-worktree}/.live/tmux/tmux-501/fm-lab (File name too long).
   Follow-up display-message and kill-server returned the same path-length error; no server started.

2. Guarded Herdr session:
   FM_HERDR_LAB_STATE_DIR=$PWD/.live/herdr-state
   S=$(bin/fm-herdr-lab.sh name placement)
   bin/fm-herdr-lab.sh prepare "$S" && bin/fm-herdr-lab.sh provision "$S" && bin/fm-herdr-lab.sh run "$S" status --json
   Result: fm-herdr-lab: fleet-state tripwire requires exactly one running default session.
   prepare failed, so provision and status did not run. No Herdr session was created or changed. The live default session was not started or manipulated.

3. Alternative isolation assessment:
   docker info --format '{{.ServerVersion}}' returned 29.5.2.
   docker image ls showed Python, Postgres, OpenBao verification, gateway and hello-world images, but no prepared Firstmate/harness worker image. No container was started or production credentials copied. A container would additionally need a Linux harness runtime and its own authorized login; the existing macOS login was not copied.
   The real SSH entrypoint explicitly resolves the account's actual login home and bootstraps its remote job worker there. Thus changing FM_HOME alone does not isolate that service, and running it against the operator account would violate the workspace boundary.

4. Successful bounded fallback:
   The real fm-capacity, fm-placement, fm-spawn, tasks-axi and destination-side fm-remote-worker-control CLIs were exercised directly against two marked workspace-local lab homes. An actual OpenSSH connection to 127.0.0.1:1 used an isolated -F config, IdentityFile none, IdentityAgent none, and local known-hosts path. It failed without authentication; no remote success is claimed. See live-cli.log and live-driver.sh.

The final live-driver.sh run exited 0. Earlier setup attempts omitted a pending task's brief/backlog item; both omissions were repaired using fm-brief and tasks-axi before the final run. Those were fixture errors, not product failures.
